"""Concise culture-negative and antimicrobial teaching, retaining named frameworks."""
from cardiology_10_compact_diagnosis import B,S
edits={
'10-031':S('Dual Beta-Lactam Synergy',[B('**Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone targets complementary PBPs in E. faecalis.**','Useful with high-level gentamicin resistance and avoids aminoglycoside renal toxicity.'),B('**Usual normal-clearance adult regimen:** ampicillin ==2 g IV every 4 hours== plus ceftriaxone ==2 g IV every 12 hours== for 6 weeks.','Adjust ampicillin for renal function; assess fluid/sodium burden. Ceftriaxone frequency differs from streptococcal/HACEK monotherapy.')],
 'Resistance and Follow-Up',[B('**High-level gentamicin resistance abolishes expected synergy.**','Increasing gentamicin exposure does not safely restore it.'),B('**Confirm species and ampicillin susceptibility.**','Do not extrapolate this regimen to ampicillin-resistant E. faecium.'),B('**Follow cultures, blood counts, renal/hepatic function and anatomy.**','Persistent bacteremia requires source-control review despite an appropriate combination.')]),
'10-032':S('ESC 2023 Resistant-Enterococcus Strategy',[B('**Daptomycin 10–12 mg/kg IV every 24 hours** with a selected beta-lactam or fosfomycin companion.','Confirm species, MIC interpretation and achievable exposure; renal dysfunction can change delivery intervals.'),B('**A beta-lactam may contribute combination activity despite inadequate standalone activity.**','The choice is isolate-specific, not interchangeable across drugs.')],
 'Monitor Treatment and Alternatives',[B('**Follow CK, renal function and muscle symptoms.**','Review other myotoxic drugs; investigate new respiratory symptoms for drug toxicity.'),B('**Linezolid is an alternative in selected circumstances.**','Prolonged treatment requires blood-count, interaction and neuropathy surveillance.'),B('**Evidence is limited; assess surgery and metastatic foci.**','Neither susceptibility nor a high dose compensates for uncontrolled infection.')]),
'10-033':S('Ceftriaxone and Valve-Specific Duration',[B('**HACEK PVE:** ceftriaxone ==2 g IV daily for 6 weeks==.','Native-valve infection generally uses 4 weeks; clearance does not justify a short bacteremia course.'),B('**HACEK:** Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella and Kingella.','Beta-lactamase production limits unqualified ampicillin use.')],
 'Alternative Regimens and Assessment',[B('**Ampicillin-based treatment requires appropriate isolate characterization.**','Ciprofloxacin is a less validated alternative when susceptibility and clinical circumstances support it.'),B('**Modern cultures can recover HACEK organisms.**','Do not assign this cause merely because routine cultures are negative.'),B('**Assess prosthetic function, extension and emboli.**','Organism identity alone neither establishes uncomplicated disease nor mandates valve replacement.')]),
'10-034':S('Initial Antifungal and Surgical Plan',[B('**Liposomal amphotericin B 3–5 mg/kg IV daily**, with or without flucytosine ==25 mg/kg every 6 hours==.','Adjust flucytosine for renal function and monitor concentrations when available.'),B('**An appropriate echinocandin regimen is an alternative**, e.g. micafungin 150 mg IV daily.','Do not assume every candidemia dose is an endocarditis dose.'),B('**Early surgery when feasible:** 2025 global guidance favors the first week or earlier if required.')],
 'Completion and Monitoring',[B('**Continue antifungals ≥6 weeks after surgery**, longer for complications.','This differs from bacterial-IE duration counting.'),B('**Fluconazole 400–800 mg daily** can be selected for step-down after susceptibility, stability and clearance requirements are met.','Inoperable disease may require prolonged or lifelong suppression.'),B('**Monitor renal function/electrolytes, blood counts, hepatic toxicity and relapse.**','Initial fluconazole monotherapy is not justified solely by susceptibility.')]),
'10-035':S('Integrated Non-HACEK Gram-Negative Care',[B('**ESC: early surgical assessment plus prolonged bactericidal combination therapy**, usually 6 weeks.','Select an active beta-lactam and companion by organism, resistance mechanism, exposure and toxicity.'),B('**Serratia PVE is not a routine HACEK or uncomplicated bacteremia scenario.**')],
 'Individualize the Regimen',[B('**Review susceptibility and emerging resistance risk with microbiology.**','Aminoglycoside or selected alternative partners require drug-specific monitoring; no universal combination is proven superior.'),B('**Assess prosthetic stability, abscess, emboli and persistent bacteremia.**','If surgery is prohibitive, document an individualized plan and close surveillance.'),B('**POET cannot be routinely extrapolated to Serratia PVE.**','Clearance alone does not establish oral-switch eligibility.')]),
'10-036':S('Initial Healthcare-Associated Spectrum',[B('**Cover methicillin-resistant staphylococci, enterococci and relevant non-HACEK Gram-negative organisms.**','Coagulase-negative staphylococci matter in early prosthetic infection.'),B('**Select actual drugs by local resistance, prior cultures/antibiotics, allergy and organ function.**','A spectrum description is not a universal multidrug prescription.')],
 'Cultures to Definitive Therapy',[B('**Prompt cultures → empirical treatment → identification/susceptibility → targeted regimen.**','Shock treatment must not wait for the stable-patient sampling timetable.'),B('**Assess dehiscence, abscess and severe dysfunction early.**','Antibiotics do not replace indicated source control.'),B('**Persistent negative cultures warrant directed investigation.**','Avoid indefinite broadening; rifampin timing is a separate organism-specific decision.')]),
'10-037':S('ESC 2023 Duration Anchor',[B('**Count effective therapy using culture clearance when initially bacteremic.**','The supplied sustained clearance begins on day 3; surgery on day 12 does not automatically restart treatment.'),B('**ESC starts a new full course for positive valve cultures.**','Negative treated valve cultures do not justify stopping early; molecular positivity differs from viable growth.')],
 'Guideline and Infection-Specific Differences',[B('**AHA 2026 credits preoperative days with ≥14 postoperative days when therapy is unfinished.**','It does not reset solely for positive valve culture/PCR; retain the named framework in interpretation.'),B('**Residual abscess or metastatic infection can alter duration.**','A new prosthesis during NVE treatment does not automatically create a PVE regimen.'),B('**Candida uses a distinct postoperative duration rule.**','Document the clearance date, planned completion and reasons for extension.')]),
'10-024':S('Full Beta-Lactam Course',[B('**Susceptible oral-streptococcal NVE:** ceftriaxone ==2 g IV every 24 hours for 4 weeks==.','Susceptible PVE ordinarily requires 6 weeks; suitable penicillin/amoxicillin regimens are alternatives.'),B('**Two-week monotherapy is not the short-course strategy.**','Selected abbreviated treatment requires a synergistic combination and strict eligibility.')],
 'Renal Risk and Follow-Up',[B('**CKD favors avoiding unnecessary aminoglycoside exposure.**','Monitor cultures, blood counts, organ function and ceftriaxone adverse effects, including biliary complications.'),B('**Reassess anatomy throughout treatment.**','Abscess, embolic complications or valve failure change management; susceptibility alone does not establish uncomplicated disease.')]),
'10-025':S('Interpret Drug-Specific Susceptibility',[B('**Penicillin-intermediate does not necessarily mean ceftriaxone-resistant.**','The 2026 AHA writing group supports a full ceftriaxone course without obligatory gentamicin in selected ceftriaxone-susceptible VGS/S. gallolyticus IE.'),B('**Ceftriaxone:** usual adult dose ==2 g IV daily==.','Uncomplicated NVE generally uses 4 weeks; PVE generally 6 weeks, with complications assessed separately.')],
 'Limits of the Update',[B('**The evidence is largely observational and consensus-based.**','Do not extrapolate to untested or fully resistant isolates; use the laboratory breakpoint framework.'),B('**Avoiding gentamicin is not permission to shorten monotherapy to 2 weeks.**','Monitor clearance, anatomy and adverse effects; fully resistant infections require a separate regimen assessment.')]),
'10-026':S('Short-Course Eligibility',[B('**Susceptible oral streptococcus + uncomplicated NVE + normal renal function.**','Exclude prosthetic infection, local invasion and important extracardiac complications.'),B('**Example 2-week combination:** ceftriaxone ==2 g IV daily== plus gentamicin ==3 mg/kg/day==, commonly once daily.','Individualize aminoglycoside dosing to weight, clearance and drug concentrations.')],
 'Safety and Alternatives',[B('**Monitor renal, auditory and vestibular toxicity.**','Rapid defervescence alone does not establish eligibility.'),B('**Four-week active beta-lactam monotherapy** is a standard alternative.','Do not extend the short-course rule to enterococci, staphylococci or nutritionally variant streptococci; outpatient treatment requires stability and reliable monitoring.')]),
'10-027':S('Target MSSA With a Beta-Lactam',[B('**Normal-renal-function adult examples:** cloxacillin/oxacillin ==2 g IV every 4 hours== or cefazolin ==2 g IV every 8 hours==.','Select by allergy phenotype, availability and organ function; renal impairment changes cefazolin dosing.'),B('**Left-sided staphylococcal NVE:** usually 4–6 weeks.','The restricted short-course right-sided pathway does not apply.')],
 'Adjuncts and Monitoring',[B('**Routine gentamicin or rifampin is not indicated in this native-valve setting.**','Do not import foreign-material or streptococcal synergy rules.'),B('**Monitor blood counts, renal/hepatic function and cultures.**','Penicillins can cause interstitial nephritis, hepatic injury, electrolyte disturbance and cytopenias.'),B('**Persistent bacteremia requires source reassessment**, including metastatic infection and surgical indications.')]),
'10-028':S('AUC-Guided Vancomycin',[B('**Target AUC24/MIC 400–600** for serious MRSA infection when MIC is 1 mg/L.','This corresponds to ==AUC24 400–600 mg·h/L==; use validated Bayesian or concentration-based pharmacokinetic estimation.'),B('**Assess early and after renal or clinical changes.**','Trough 15–20 mg/L alone is an older surrogate that can increase nephrotoxicity.')],
 'Persistent Infection',[B('**Check delivery, susceptibility and source control.**','Do not simply raise exposure beyond the safe range to compensate for a higher MIC.'),B('**Selected alternatives include high-dose daptomycin.**','ESC commonly uses 10 mg/kg IV daily with a selected companion; monitor CK and renal function.'),B('**Continue clearance cultures and anatomical assessment.**','An exposure target does not replace treatment duration or indicated surgery.')]),
'10-029':S('ESC 2023 Rifampin Timing',[B('**Introduce after roughly 3–5 days of effective therapy, once bacteremia clears.**','Persistent positive cultures override the calendar; defervescence is not proof of clearance.'),B('**Typical PVE regimen:** rifampin ==900–1200 mg/day== in divided doses for ≥6 weeks.','Use active companion therapy; monotherapy selects resistance.')],
 'Evidence and Interactions',[B('**Evidence for adjunctive rifampin is limited and contested.**','Review hepatotoxicity and enzyme-induction interactions, including anticoagulants.'),B('**AHA 2026 did not reach rifampin consensus and advises against gentamicin in staphylococcal PVE.**','The stem specifically tests ESC timing; do not describe it as universal agreement.'),B('**Assess prosthetic stability, invasion and surgical source control** alongside antibiotics.')]),
'10-030':S('AHA 2026 Gentamicin Position',[B('**Risks outweigh potential benefit in staphylococcal PVE.**','Observational studies and pooled analyses have not established a convincing advantage sufficient to offset toxicity.'),B('**Renal, auditory and vestibular injury remain concerns.**','Normal baseline creatinine does not make an unnecessary adjunct harmless.')],
 'Separate the Rifampin Decision',[B('**No AHA writing-group consensus on rifampin.**','Weigh biofilm rationale against interactions, hepatotoxicity, resistance and limited clinical evidence.'),B('**Maintain an effective primary regimen and evaluate surgery.**','Assess valve dysfunction, abscess and prosthetic stability; dropping gentamicin does not replace source control.'),B('**Organism-specific conclusion:** this does not remove valid aminoglycoside roles in other infections.')]),
'10-018':S('Interpret the Antibiotic History',[B('**Prior antibiotics can suppress ordinary IE bacteria.**','Retrieve agents, doses, timing and pre-treatment cultures; negative treated samples differ from adequately sampled untreated blood.'),B('**Untreated culture-negative disease** warrants organism-directed investigation guided by exposures and valve setting.')],
 'Refine Diagnosis and Treatment',[B('**No single empirical BCNE regimen fits every patient.**','Consider acquisition, valve type, resistance risk, previous treatment and organ function.'),B('**Coordinate cultures, targeted serology/PCR and operative tissue testing.**','Do not interrupt necessary treatment in unstable or complicated disease merely to improve yield.'),B('**Review sterile mimics when the phenotype warrants**, not solely because treated cultures are negative.')]),
'10-019':S('Expand Testing at About 72 Hours',[B('**AHA 2025:** review persistent negative cultures with microbiology.','Check volume, antibiotic exposure, special culture requirements and selected extended incubation.'),B('**Add Coxiella/Bartonella serology and directed molecular tests.**','Consider Brucella with relevant geography, animal contact or unpasteurized dairy exposure.')],
 'Use Complementary Evidence',[B('**Exposure history guides testing:** animals, body lice, housing conditions, travel, surgery and implants.','Negative blood PCR may miss tissue-predominant infection; broad molecular tests can detect contaminants.'),B('**Plan tissue culture, histology and PCR if surgery occurs.**','Do not delay treatment of HF, abscess or another source-control indication while awaiting send-away results.')]),
'10-020':S('Persistent Focal Q Fever',[B('**Valve or vascular infection can be indolent and culture negative.**','Pre-existing valve disease and livestock exposure support investigation.'),B('**Duke–ISCVID major threshold:** phase-I IgG ==>1:800==.','The 1:3200 result qualifies, but serology must accompany a compatible focal syndrome.')],
 'Prolonged Treatment and Monitoring',[B('**CDC adult regimen:** doxycycline ==100 mg every 12 hours== plus hydroxychloroquine ==200 mg every 8 hours== for at least 18 months.','Prosthetic or difficult focal infection may require longer specialist-directed treatment; acute-Q-fever courses are insufficient.'),B('**Follow clinical response, serology, interactions and retinal toxicity.**','One titre is not an automatic stopping rule; assess valve failure or uncontrolled infection for surgery and monitor relapse.')]),
'10-021':S('Exposure and Confirmation',[B('**B. quintana:** body lice; **B. henselae:** cats/kittens and fleas.','Either can cause culture-negative IE with immune-complex renal injury.'),B('**Serology plus targeted blood/tissue PCR.**','Qualifying IgG ≥1:800 or blood nucleic-acid detection provides major evidence; cross-reactivity and negative blood-PCR sensitivity limit interpretation.')],
 'Treatment and Source Assessment',[B('**AHA 2025:** doxycycline ==100 mg every 12 hours== for 12 weeks, with rifampin for the first 6 weeks.','Rifampin 300 mg every 12 hours is a recognized adult combination dose; NIH HIV guidance has separate continuation requirements.'),B('**Monitor liver tests, interactions, renal disease and response.**','Aminoglycoside renal toxicity is particularly relevant with glomerulonephritis.'),B('**Treat infestation and assess valve dysfunction/source control.**','Lice eradication or renal improvement alone does not establish cure of IE.')]),
'10-022':S('Whipple Disease Without Diarrhea',[B('**Migratory arthralgia can precede culture-negative valve disease.**','HF and regurgitation may dominate; absent malabsorption does not exclude infection.'),B('**Targeted blood PCR; valve PCR/histology when available.**','Negative blood PCR does not exclude tissue-predominant disease; stool/saliva positivity may reflect carriage.')],
 'Keep Treatment Frameworks Separate',[B('**ESC 2023:** doxycycline ==200 mg/day== plus hydroxychloroquine ==200–600 mg/day== for at least 18 months.'),B('**AHA 2025:** initial 4-week IV phase, e.g. ceftriaxone ==2 g daily==, then ≥11 months of appropriate oral maintenance.','These are distinct approaches, not a combined schedule.'),B('**Assess CNS involvement, relapse and drug/retinal toxicity.**','Evidence is limited; specialist selection and treatment of valve failure, including indicated surgery, remain essential.')]),
'10-023':S('Allocate Tissue Before Fixation',[B('**Coordinate surgery, microbiology and pathology in advance.**','Provide antibiotics, exposure history and suspected pathogens; label representative tissue/prosthetic material appropriately.'),B('**Preserve unfixed sterile material for required microbial tests and a separate histology portion.**','Follow the receiving laboratory’s transport protocol; formalin prevents conventional viable-organism culture.')],
 'Interpret the Combined Results',[B('**Culture:** organism and susceptibility; **histology:** active inflammation/mimics; **molecular tests:** organism evidence despite culture suppression.','Select fungal/mycobacterial tests or special stains by clinical probability.'),B('**DNA can persist after treatment and contamination can mislead.**','An isolated skin-organism PCR signal without corroboration is not automatic pathological definite IE. Negative treated tissue culture does not erase convincing operative or histological evidence.')]),
}
